  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 PM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The heat and humidity will continue into late week, \nwith a slight break over the weekend then returning early next week.\n\nFor the rest of the week, Thursday and Friday, Central NC will \nremain on the northern edge of a sub-tropical ridge, with a \npersistent surface trough driving continued extreme heat. While some \nscattered afternoon convection is possible, highs will reach the low \n90s north to near 100 degrees in the south. Combining these temps \nwith high humidity, expect heat indices of 100 to 108. A Heat \nAdvisory has been issued for areas from Raleigh to the south and \neast for Thursday, and one may also be needed Friday for the \nSandhills and Coastal Plain.\n\nOver the weekend, temperatures will moderate slightly as high \npressure slides off the Mid-Atlantic coast. This will generate a \nweak northeasterly surface flow behind the front and result in near-\nnormal highs. However, lingering low-level moisture will maintain \ntriple digit heat indices, while passing shortwaves continue to \nforce typical isolated to scattered convection both afternoons.\n\nInto the early and middle of next week oppressive heat returns as \nthe upper-level ridge shifts east and the next incoming trough \nstrengthens. Depending on how afternoon dew points mix out each day, \nheat index values will peak again up to 109 degrees for parts of the \narea. This secondary wave of high heat indices will likely require \nadditional Heat Advisories for much of central NC early to mid week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... The chance for mostly diurnal convection persists \ninto early next week, but with low confidence in the placement and \ntiming details. \n\nThe overall central/E CONUS pattern will remain largely the same \nheading into the weekend, featuring a quasi-stationary NW-to-SE \nfrontal zone (which could actually waver significantly in spots \nbased on the whims of convective complexes and outflows) from the \nMidwest through Ohio Valley and NC. This front will be aligned with \na swift and perturbed NW steering flow from the Upper Midwest \nthrough the Mid Atlantic, on the N and NE side of a building mid \nlevel ridge covering the interior Southeast, Gulf States, and lower \nMiss Valley. This will put central NC within the path of potentially \nmultiple MCS remnants or MCVs tracking ESE or SE into our area over \nthe next few days, all with poor predictability beyond 12-24 hrs. \nThrough tonight, a mature convective complex now over S/E IN and W \nOH is expected to weaken as it continues pushing generally SE, but \nmay still spawn a few showers and perhaps an isolated storm across \nour extreme W and S between late this afternoon into the early \novernight hours. Deep layer bulk shear is expected to be 20-25 kts \nthrough tonight, but lingering 500-750 J/kg of CAPE will be \nincreasingly overwhelmed by nocturnal CINH. Regardless of whether or \nnot any convection survives this far SE, the veil of debris mid and \nhigh clouds will certainly pass overhead into Thu morning. \n\nConfidence remains pretty good that the mid level pattern and \nsurface frontal pattern will be maintained for at least a few days, \nagain with deviations in frontal position, and we'll stay within a \nsteamy air mass featuring a weak W to NW low level flow and above-\nnormal high temps in the 90s. So any convection will be able to tap \ninto this juicy air mass with late-day CAPE peaking near 1000-1500 \nJ/kg. But the mid level lapse rates will stay modest, and with the \nlight winds leading to a lack of distinct focus for convection, the \ncoverage is likely to stay sub-climatology, isold to scattered at \nmost, but with uncertain timing and placement. No widespread severe \nweather is expected in our area, however, for the next few days.",
